    title: "How Is Mass Timber Designed for Structural Loads?",
    description: "Mass timber structures demand engineered CLT and glulam design. How our engineers handle gravity frames, lateral systems, connections, and floor vibration.",
    h1: "How Is Mass Timber Designed for Structural Loads?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that a mass timber structure is designed as a lightweight, stiff system in which cross-laminated timber (CLT) panels carry floor and wall loads to glue-laminated (glulam) beams and columns, and the lateral system relies on CLT shear walls or glulam frames connected with ductile steel hardware. Gravity design is usually governed by serviceability — deflection and vibration — rather than strength, because wood is strong relative to its weight. Connection design is the heart of the work: I detail a hierarchy in which steel connectors yield in a controlled, ductile way before the wood itself crushes or splits.\n\nFor gravity systems I start with the panel spans. CLT floor panels typically span one direction between glulam beams, with practical spans driven by the panel thickness and the vibration criteria for the occupancy. I lay the column grid so the glulam beams land on economical spans and the CLT cantilevers only where the panel layup supports it. Bearing is checked at every support — wood crushes perpendicular to grain at much lower stress than parallel — so I design bearing plates, column caps, and sill details that spread the load into the fibers that can take it. Two-way panel action is possible with balanced layups, but I confirm it with the panel manufacturer's data rather than assuming it.\n\nThe lateral system depends on the framing type. In platform-framed CLT buildings, each story's wall panels act as shear walls tied together with hold-downs at the ends and angle brackets along the base; in glulam post-and-beam buildings, I design braced frames or moment frames with slotted-in steel plates and dowel groups. Seismic design follows the building code's wood provisions, and in high seismic design categories I keep the structure's ductility in the connections — the steel yields, the timber stays elastic. Shrinkage is a mass timber reality I design for from day one: I detail slip connections and tolerance gaps so a few millimeters of cross-grain shrinkage do not drag the lateral system out of alignment.\n\nJurisdiction matters more than most owners expect. Some building departments are fluent in mass timber and review it routinely; others treat the first mass timber submittal as an alternate-materials review with longer plan-check cycles. I coordinate early with the authority having jurisdiction on the construction type classification, the fire strategy for the timber, and whether the panel manufacturer's test data will be accepted at face value. Early coordination is the difference between a clean review and a second round of structural comments.",
