    title: "How Is MEP Coordinated in Modular Building Construction?",
    description: "Modular buildings move MEP rough-in into the factory. How our engineers coordinate module-to-module connections, commissioning splits, and site tie-ins.",
    h1: "How Is MEP Coordinated in Modular Building Construction?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that MEP in modular construction is designed twice: once for the finished building, and once for the factory floor, where every duct run, pipe, and conduit must terminate at a module boundary with a connection point that can be joined quickly and reliably on site. I design the systems so that each module ships as close to finished as possible — rough-in complete, devices mounted, systems pressure-tested at the factory — and the site work is reduced to stitching modules together at planned interconnection points. The coordination model is unforgiving: a pipe stub that misses its mate by an inch in the field cannot be nudged the way site-built work can.\n\nModule-to-module connections are the critical design element. I locate every interconnection — duct joints, pipe couplings, electrical bus connections, data terminations — on the coordination drawings with exact dimensions from the module datum, and I specify connection types made for blind or tight-access joining: grooved couplings, flanged duct joints with gasketed access panels, and plug-and-play electrical connectors where the code allows them. Chases are aligned vertically through stacked modules so risers drop straight through, and I hold clear zones at every module edge so the site crew has working room to make the joints. Penetrations through module walls and floors get fire-stopping and acoustic details designed for the joint, not the monolithic wall the code examples assume.\n\nCommissioning is split between factory and field, and I write that split into the commissioning plan. Factory testing covers what the factory can prove — pressure tests on piping, megger tests on wiring, duct leakage on assembled runs — with witnessed test reports that travel with the module. Site commissioning then covers the interconnections and the whole-building performance: I require re-testing of every field joint, full-system startup, and functional performance testing of the integrated building. The building does not get its final sign-off from a stack of factory test reports alone.\n\nPermitting a modular project means navigating two jurisdictions' worth of process: the factory's certification program and the site's building department. Factory-built modules typically carry a state or third-party certification label, and the site permit covers foundations, module setting, interconnections, and site utilities. I keep the drawing set organized so the plan checker can see exactly which work is factory-certified and which work is field-verified — that clarity is what keeps a modular permit moving instead of stalling in questions.",
