    slug: "cross-laminated-timber-fire-rating",
    title: "How Does Cross-Laminated Timber Achieve a Fire Rating?",
    description: "CLT earns fire ratings through predictable charring or encapsulation. How our engineers use char rates, protective layers, and tested assemblies for compliance.",
    h1: "How Does Cross-Laminated Timber Achieve a Fire Rating?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that cross-laminated timber achieves a fire rating in one of two ways: by exposing the timber and oversizing the members so a calculated char layer sacrifices itself while the remaining wood keeps carrying the load, or by encapsulating the timber behind fire-protective layers like gypsum board so the wood never sees the fire. Both paths are recognized by the building code, and the choice is driven by the construction type, the required rating, and how much exposed wood the architecture wants. I never treat fire design as a coating or a guess — it is a calculation with a published char rate and a defined residual section.\n\nThe char method works because wood chars at a slow, predictable rate — roughly two-thirds of a millimeter per minute under standard fire exposure — and the char layer insulates the wood behind it. I calculate the char depth for the required fire duration, subtract it (plus a zero-strength zone just behind the char front) from the member, and verify that the remaining cross-section still carries the fire-load combination. Adhesive integrity matters enormously here: only CLT manufactured with heat-resistant adhesives qualified under the product standard maintains its lamella bond in a fire, because a delaminating panel sheds its char layer and exposes fresh wood. I specify qualified panels and confirm the certification with the manufacturer.\n\nEncapsulation is the other path, and it is required by the construction types that allow the tallest mass timber buildings. Layers of Type X gypsum board are installed to a tested assembly detail, and the rating comes from the assembly's tested performance — the fastening pattern, the joint treatment, and the layer count all matter. Partial encapsulation is a legitimate middle ground on some projects: protect the connections and the most exposed faces, expose the rest, and let the char calculation cover the exposed portions. Concealed spaces need attention either way, because fire and smoke traveling unseen through a floor or wall cavity is a failure of the design, not of the material.\n\nPlan-check expectations for mass timber fire design vary widely by jurisdiction. Some building departments accept the char calculation with the manufacturer's adhesive qualification as a matter of routine; others want to see a listed assembly or a fire protection engineer's peer review, especially on the first mass timber project in that jurisdiction. I find out which during schematic design — not during plan check — and I coordinate the structural fire design with the project's fire protection engineer so the suppression, detection, and passive strategies tell one consistent story.",
