        body: "I run the char calculation the way the wood design standards intend: start with the published one-dimensional char rate, multiply by the required fire duration, add the zero-strength layer behind the char front, and check the residual section under the fire-load combination. The fire-load combination is lighter than the design loads — the code recognizes that a building at full design occupancy during a fully developed fire is not the scenario being designed for — so members that look thin for everyday loads can still pass the fire check. I document the calculation member by member, because the plan checker will ask for it and the exercise catches the members where the architecture's exposed-timber ambitions exceed the rating the section can deliver.\n\nPanel layup affects the result. A five-ply panel of a given thickness outperforms a three-ply panel of the same thickness in a fire, because more of the section remains in the strong axis after charring. I coordinate the layup with the manufacturer early — changing ply count late in the project ripples through the gravity design, the connection design, and the cost. Edges and ends get the same treatment as faces: I detail edge protection where panels meet, because fire finds the joint the designer forgot.",
