        heading: "Wind design and anchorage",
        body: "I design the safe room for the design wind speed mapped to the site's tornado hazard, up to 250 mph, which produces pressures an order of magnitude beyond conventional design. The walls and roof are engineered as a rigid box: I check the wall panels for the windward pressure and the leeward and side-wall suctions simultaneously, and I design the roof for the extreme uplift at its edges and corners. The box must act compositely, so the wall-to-roof and wall-to-foundation connections are designed for the full shear and tension — a safe room assembled from strong panels with weak joints fails at the joints.\n\nAnchorage is where retrofit projects most often need real engineering. The overturning from a 250 mph wind on even a small room is enormous, and residential slabs and foundations were never designed for it. I design new footings or underpinning that develop the full hold-down force, with anchor bolts or post-installed anchors qualified for the cracked-concrete, seismic-type loading the event will produce. The anchorage design is documented and inspected — a safe room whose hold-downs were never verified is a hypothesis, not a shelter.",
